WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.534 --> 00:00:03.369
-Our first guest tonight is
a Grammy Award-winning musician,

00:00:03.369 --> 00:00:06.205
multi-platinum recording artist,
and actor.

00:00:06.205 --> 00:00:08.008
He stars in "X,"
which is in theaters

00:00:08.008 --> 00:00:09.809
nationwide this Friday,
March 18th.

00:00:09.809 --> 00:00:11.043
Let's take a look.

00:00:11.043 --> 00:00:13.379 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:13.379 --> 00:00:16.949
-Sorry to bother you, ma'am,
but my van broke down

00:00:16.949 --> 00:00:19.152
on the highway back yonder,
and I was hoping

00:00:19.152 --> 00:00:23.356
I could use y'all's phone
to call for help.

00:00:23.356 --> 00:00:25.224
-Oh, my goodness.

00:00:25.224 --> 00:00:27.059
I'm afraid
we don't own a telephone,

00:00:27.059 --> 00:00:30.029
but my daddy will be home soon,

00:00:30.029 --> 00:00:33.099
and I'm sure he could
give you a lift to town.

00:00:33.099 --> 00:00:36.536
Would you like to come inside?

00:00:36.536 --> 00:00:39.338 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:39.338 --> 00:00:40.673
-Much obliged.

00:00:40.673 --> 00:00:43.676
-Please welcome to the show
Kid Cudi, everybody.

00:00:43.676 --> 00:00:45.678
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:45.678 --> 00:00:55.688 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:55.688 --> 00:00:57.257 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:57.257 --> 00:01:00.059
-Welcome to the show!
-What's good?

00:01:00.059 --> 00:01:02.295
Yeah!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:02.295 --> 00:01:05.331
Yeah!

00:01:05.331 --> 00:01:07.733
-They're very happy you're here.
I'm very happy you're here.

00:01:07.733 --> 00:01:09.569
I do --
That's a fantastic clip,

00:01:09.569 --> 00:01:13.072
but I feel like what we showed
just makes it look like

00:01:13.072 --> 00:01:14.507
an adult film from the '70s.

00:01:14.507 --> 00:01:15.841
[ Laughter ]
-Exactly.

00:01:15.841 --> 00:01:19.646
-But this is a horror film
about making an adult film

00:01:19.646 --> 00:01:22.047
in the '70s that then,
a lot of stuff goes wrong.

00:01:22.047 --> 00:01:24.183
-Yeah, it gets wild.
-And it looks beautiful!

00:01:24.183 --> 00:01:27.553
Like, is -- it's so fun to watch
a sort of period piece

00:01:27.553 --> 00:01:29.555
that's also a horror film,
and it just looks great,

00:01:29.555 --> 00:01:30.823
the whole film.

00:01:30.823 --> 00:01:32.859
-Yeah, Ti is just a genius,
and he had a vision.

00:01:32.859 --> 00:01:35.995
And, you know,
as soon as I got this e-mail

00:01:35.995 --> 00:01:38.497
about this project, I jumped
at the opportunity, you know?

00:01:38.497 --> 00:01:41.133
-It has a great location
for a horror movie,

00:01:41.133 --> 00:01:42.802
which I think
is such a key to it.

00:01:42.802 --> 00:01:44.236
-Yeah.
-It's like, sort of, an old,

00:01:44.236 --> 00:01:45.905
rundown farm.

00:01:45.905 --> 00:01:47.173
Obviously, when you're filming

00:01:47.173 --> 00:01:49.975
a horror movie, it's not
as scary as watching it.

00:01:49.975 --> 00:01:51.677
But are you --
as you're making it,

00:01:51.677 --> 00:01:52.678
do you think,
"Oh, this is gonna

00:01:52.678 --> 00:01:54.213
freak people out
when they see it"?

00:01:54.213 --> 00:01:58.584
-Yeah, like, every so often,
you have a project

00:01:58.584 --> 00:02:00.286
that you're working on,
and you feel,

00:02:00.286 --> 00:02:02.688
while you're in it, that you're
making something special.

00:02:02.688 --> 00:02:05.525
And I really felt that
with this movie.

00:02:05.525 --> 00:02:08.193
You know?
And there were some scenes --

00:02:08.193 --> 00:02:10.997
It wasn't, like, scary for me,
because this is

00:02:10.997 --> 00:02:14.066
my first horror movie,
so I don't know how it is.

00:02:14.066 --> 00:02:17.603
But I wasn't really scared,
but there was this one scene

00:02:17.603 --> 00:02:21.040
where we were out in the woods,
and it was, like, late at night.

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:22.675
It was like 1:00 in the morning,
and I was look,

00:02:22.675 --> 00:02:24.010
"Ooh, this is cool."
[ Laughter ]

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:26.345
"I'm doing, like,
classic horror-movie scene.

00:02:26.345 --> 00:02:27.780
I'm, like, in the woods.
It's creepy.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:30.150
I'm walking by myself.
Anything could happen.

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:33.152
Anything could happen!"
[ Laughter ]

00:02:33.152 --> 00:02:34.453
You know, but it was exciting,
'cause, like,

00:02:34.453 --> 00:02:35.855
I'm a huge horror fan.

00:02:35.855 --> 00:02:37.423
-And you started
watching them very young.

00:02:37.423 --> 00:02:38.657
-Very young, man.

00:02:38.657 --> 00:02:40.426
-So how old were you
when you started watching?

00:02:40.426 --> 00:02:43.162
-I think my first horror movie
was 7-years-old.

00:02:43.162 --> 00:02:44.663
-Okay.
-But I had older siblings,

00:02:44.663 --> 00:02:46.799
'cause, like,
they were always playing stuff,

00:02:46.799 --> 00:02:49.201
and I would be curious to see
what they were watching.

00:02:49.201 --> 00:02:51.370
But I think my first horror
movie that I ever watched

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:53.839
was "Night of the Living Dead,"
and then "Evil Dead,"

00:02:53.839 --> 00:02:55.241
and then Freddy Krueger, like,

00:02:55.241 --> 00:02:56.642
they were all around
the same time.

00:02:56.642 --> 00:02:58.644
-And were someone who --
were you the kind of kid

00:02:58.644 --> 00:03:01.480
who then had nightmares, or did
you just have the joy of horror?

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:02.982
Was it just entertainment?

00:03:02.982 --> 00:03:05.718
-I had nightmares.
I enjoyed being scared.

00:03:05.718 --> 00:03:07.253
-Yeah.
-You know, like --

00:03:07.253 --> 00:03:09.655
You know, I don't know,
it was just something about it.

00:03:09.655 --> 00:03:11.424
You know?
It's just like,

00:03:11.424 --> 00:03:13.092
I guess why people like
to get on roller coasters.

00:03:13.092 --> 00:03:15.160
-Yeah.
-You know, it's just like,

00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:16.695
you like putting yourself

00:03:16.695 --> 00:03:18.831
through the thrill
of being scared.

00:03:18.831 --> 00:03:20.299
-And now you're a father,
as well.

00:03:20.299 --> 00:03:22.034
-Yes.
-Do you let your child --

00:03:22.034 --> 00:03:24.803
[ Cheers and applause ]
-There you go.

00:03:24.803 --> 00:03:26.705
She's old -- how old is --
how old's your daughter?

00:03:26.705 --> 00:03:28.374
-My daughter will be 12
in a couple of weeks.

00:03:28.374 --> 00:03:29.875
-All right, and now,
were you protective of her?

00:03:29.875 --> 00:03:31.377
No horror movies for her?

00:03:31.377 --> 00:03:33.680
-No, see --
See, here's the thing, Seth.

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:34.914
-Okay, I'm very excited
about this.

00:03:34.914 --> 00:03:37.750
-When I had my child...
-Uh-huh.

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:38.984
-...I thought about
all the things

00:03:38.984 --> 00:03:40.319
I could mold her to be,
you know?

00:03:40.319 --> 00:03:41.587
[ Laughter ]

00:03:41.587 --> 00:03:43.556
Like, video games, for example.
-Okay.

00:03:43.556 --> 00:03:45.224
-I wanted her to be a gamer.
-Oh, interesting.

00:03:45.224 --> 00:03:48.795
-So, like, I put like all these
"Mario Kart" decorations

00:03:48.795 --> 00:03:51.998
in her room, like, strategically
so she would be a gamer.

00:03:51.998 --> 00:03:53.999
-Gotcha.
-And, so horror movies --

00:03:53.999 --> 00:03:55.968
-I feel like that's not a thing
you have to push kids towards.

00:03:55.968 --> 00:03:57.069
[ Laughter ]

00:03:57.069 --> 00:03:58.471
-Well, you know,
I was just, like, worried.

00:03:58.471 --> 00:04:00.740
Maybe she might not be into it,
so I wanted to make sure.

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:01.908
You know what I'm saying?
-Right, right.

00:04:01.908 --> 00:04:02.976
Lock it in, yep.
-Because, like, I want to play

00:04:02.976 --> 00:04:04.543
"Call of Duty Zombies"
with someone.

00:04:04.543 --> 00:04:06.178
You know what I mean?
[ Laughter ]

00:04:06.178 --> 00:04:10.649
So I had her watching
horror movies when she was 8.

00:04:10.649 --> 00:04:13.085
She was really curious,
and I was like, "Okay.

00:04:13.085 --> 00:04:15.788
I think you're old enough
to watch something," right?

00:04:15.788 --> 00:04:17.223
-Uh-huh.
-So I think "It"

00:04:17.223 --> 00:04:18.924
came out at that time.
-Okay.

00:04:18.924 --> 00:04:20.192
[ Audience groans ]
-Well, they say --

00:04:20.192 --> 00:04:21.294
-What?
-They say it --

00:04:21.294 --> 00:04:22.462
[ Laughter ]

00:04:22.462 --> 00:04:24.330
They say it right on the poster,
"Great for kids."

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:25.932
-I know!
And there's kids in it!

00:04:25.932 --> 00:04:27.700
-Kids are in it.
-There's kids in it.

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:30.203
-It's important
to see yourself in art.

00:04:30.203 --> 00:04:32.038
-Yeah, exactly.
Exactly.

00:04:32.038 --> 00:04:33.873
So, like,
you know, we watched "It."

00:04:33.873 --> 00:04:35.941
And, you know,
I'm looking at her, like,

00:04:35.941 --> 00:04:37.176
you know,
when Pennywise comes out,

00:04:37.176 --> 00:04:38.945
I'm just watching her reaction.

00:04:38.945 --> 00:04:42.014
And she's just like,
"Why they following that clown?"

00:04:42.014 --> 00:04:43.048
[ Laughter ]

00:04:43.048 --> 00:04:44.450
"Why they following
that balloon?

00:04:44.450 --> 00:04:46.686
They're stupid.
They're stupid."

00:04:46.686 --> 00:04:48.454
And I just love, like,
sitting with her,

00:04:48.454 --> 00:04:52.091
because she has these
commentaries during the movies.

00:04:52.091 --> 00:04:54.961
-Yeah.
-And she says the realest stuff.

00:04:54.961 --> 00:04:56.829
It's just like,
"Yeah, I feel you, kid."

00:04:56.829 --> 00:04:59.298
-Well, that's great.
As a father, it must be nice

00:04:59.298 --> 00:05:01.767
to hear her think the kids
in "It" are stupid, right?

00:05:01.767 --> 00:05:02.768
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah!

00:05:02.768 --> 00:05:03.769
-The worst thing
is if she's like,

00:05:03.769 --> 00:05:05.170
"Well, you gotta
follow the clown.

00:05:05.170 --> 00:05:06.572
You can't blame them."
[ Laughter ]

00:05:06.572 --> 00:05:09.742
-Well, my buddy -- my buddy Ivan
was telling me, he was like,

00:05:09.742 --> 00:05:11.744
"Well, she's a Chicago kid.
She ain't no slouch."

00:05:11.744 --> 00:05:13.011
[ Laughter ]
You know what I'm saying?

00:05:13.011 --> 00:05:14.213
Like, it's cool, man.

00:05:14.213 --> 00:05:15.547
I know she's not, like,
a chump out here.

00:05:15.547 --> 00:05:17.917
-So you're an Ohioan,
and you actually

00:05:17.917 --> 00:05:19.251
got to go to the Super Bowl.

00:05:19.251 --> 00:05:20.419
You performed at the afterparty
for the Super Bowl.

00:05:20.419 --> 00:05:23.522
-Yes, yes.
-And -- but you recently,

00:05:23.522 --> 00:05:25.925
I guess, got to know
Joe Burrow a little bit.

00:05:25.925 --> 00:05:28.794
-Yes.
-And, you know, a very --

00:05:28.794 --> 00:05:30.863
a very cool operator.

00:05:30.863 --> 00:05:32.631
The man knows what he's doing.

00:05:32.631 --> 00:05:35.368
A very young man who's obviously
incredibly accomplished already.

00:05:35.368 --> 00:05:37.570
And you reached out.
And how did this work?

00:05:37.570 --> 00:05:43.342
You asked him for a jersey?
-Well, before the Chiefs game,

00:05:43.342 --> 00:05:45.911
I reached out to him
and just told him good luck

00:05:45.911 --> 00:05:50.649
and sent him a song to kind of
get him pumped up for the game.

00:05:50.649 --> 00:05:52.551
And then, like, when they won --
-And this was one of your --

00:05:52.551 --> 00:05:53.619
a preexisting song.

00:05:53.619 --> 00:05:55.387
You didn't, like,
write him a new song.

00:05:55.387 --> 00:05:58.491
-No, I wrote him a song.
I wrote him a love song.

00:05:58.491 --> 00:06:00.092
[ Laughter ]

00:06:00.092 --> 00:06:01.927
-I just like him in the locker
room being like, "Okay.

00:06:01.927 --> 00:06:04.096
This is not what I --"
-Yeah, yeah!

00:06:04.096 --> 00:06:08.167
And it was like...
♪ Joe, hello ♪

00:06:08.167 --> 00:06:12.004
♪ You need to know
you got the glow, Joe ♪

00:06:12.004 --> 00:06:14.306
It was something like that.
-I mean, obviously, it worked.

00:06:14.306 --> 00:06:16.309
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:16.309 --> 00:06:18.211
It worked!
-So they won the game

00:06:18.211 --> 00:06:19.745
because of my song, you know?
-Yeah!

00:06:19.745 --> 00:06:21.780
And then, obviously, you forgot
to do one for the Super Bowl.

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:23.015
-Yeah, I forgot --
I forgot, yeah.

00:06:23.015 --> 00:06:24.249
[ Laughter ]

00:06:24.249 --> 00:06:27.419
But I will say that, like,
I kind of -- I got bold,

00:06:27.419 --> 00:06:28.854
and I was just like, "Man,
I'm just gonna go ahead

00:06:28.854 --> 00:06:30.489
and ask him
for the jersey, man.

00:06:30.489 --> 00:06:32.591
Like, I think maybe he might
want to keep it for himself,

00:06:32.591 --> 00:06:34.226
but let me just
go ahead and ask."

00:06:34.226 --> 00:06:36.496
And he literally responded
right away.

00:06:36.496 --> 00:06:37.830
He's like,
"I'm sending it tomorrow."

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:39.765
-And now this is a game-worn
AFC Championship jersey.

00:06:39.765 --> 00:06:41.567
-Yeah.
-And let me tell you,

00:06:41.567 --> 00:06:43.269
that's got the right amount
of grime on it.

00:06:43.269 --> 00:06:44.637
You know what I mean?
-Yeah!

00:06:44.637 --> 00:06:48.040
It's got blood stains on it.
It's real in the gridiron.

00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:49.541
-Everything about it
is outstanding.

00:06:49.541 --> 00:06:50.776
-Yeah.
-I really think that's a --

00:06:50.776 --> 00:06:52.577
that's a great thing.

00:06:52.577 --> 00:06:55.280
A great piece of sports
memorabilia for you to have.

00:06:55.280 --> 00:06:56.816
So this is very exciting.

00:06:56.816 --> 00:06:59.318
I think you're new friends
with our next guest, Quinta.

00:06:59.318 --> 00:07:01.721
-Yes.
-And she is coming out.

00:07:01.721 --> 00:07:03.088
She's got a fantastic show,
"Abbott Elementary."

00:07:03.088 --> 00:07:04.256
-Yes, she does.
-You're a fan of this show?

00:07:04.256 --> 00:07:05.457
-Yes, yes, yes.

00:07:05.457 --> 00:07:06.725
-You'd like to maybe even
be on this show.

00:07:06.725 --> 00:07:09.295
-Yeah.
And that was something that

00:07:09.295 --> 00:07:12.131
I was kind of just
throwing out with --

00:07:12.131 --> 00:07:13.699
talking to Tyler about.

00:07:13.699 --> 00:07:15.034
-Tyler, one of the co-stars
on "Abbott," yeah.

00:07:15.034 --> 00:07:19.472
-Yeah, yeah.
And, you know,

00:07:19.472 --> 00:07:22.542
I'm a big fan of teachers.
And --

00:07:22.542 --> 00:07:23.809
Like, my mom was a teacher.

00:07:23.809 --> 00:07:25.344
She was a music teacher
for 35 years.

00:07:25.344 --> 00:07:26.746
She was actually
my music teacher.

00:07:26.746 --> 00:07:27.880
[ Cheers and applause ]
-That's fantastic.

00:07:27.880 --> 00:07:30.182
-Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:07:30.182 --> 00:07:33.185
-My mom was a French teacher
for about 30 years.

00:07:33.185 --> 00:07:34.553
She was my French teacher.
-Wow!

00:07:34.553 --> 00:07:36.789
-And I hate to say it,
but based on your music career

00:07:36.789 --> 00:07:38.724
and my French career, I think
your mom was the better teacher.

00:07:38.724 --> 00:07:41.493
[ Laughter ]

00:07:41.493 --> 00:07:44.096
-Well, you know what?
We didn't say we were gonna

00:07:44.096 --> 00:07:45.231
talk about this, but I'm gonna
just put this out there.

00:07:45.231 --> 00:07:46.532
-Yeah.
-So this is --

00:07:46.532 --> 00:07:49.235
My mom was my teacher
in the seventh grade, right?

00:07:49.235 --> 00:07:53.639
And I was forced into her class,
because I knew

00:07:53.639 --> 00:07:55.674
when I went to her school,
I was like, "All right.

00:07:55.674 --> 00:07:58.544
I'm gonna have to either
be in her class or be in band,

00:07:58.544 --> 00:08:00.679
and I don't want
to be in her class,

00:08:00.679 --> 00:08:03.549
so I have to learn
how to play an instrument."

00:08:03.549 --> 00:08:06.585
So, like, I picked the trumpet
because my older brother

00:08:06.585 --> 00:08:08.454
played the trumpet,
and at that age, I was just,

00:08:08.454 --> 00:08:09.922
like, trying to do anything
he was doing.

00:08:09.922 --> 00:08:12.824
I was trying to be like him.
So I picked up the trumpet,

00:08:12.824 --> 00:08:16.194
and then, like, I was failing
this class bad, right?

00:08:16.194 --> 00:08:19.765
So, like, the teacher goes up
to my mom and he says,

00:08:19.765 --> 00:08:22.602
"Hey, your son's not doing too
good in this class, you know?

00:08:22.602 --> 00:08:23.702
And maybe you might
want to switch him out

00:08:23.702 --> 00:08:25.971
and put him in choir."

00:08:25.971 --> 00:08:28.340
So we switched me out,
and I got in choir, and, like,

00:08:28.340 --> 00:08:30.743
all year long, I'm just, like,
in the back of the class,

00:08:30.743 --> 00:08:32.411
trying not to get called on.

00:08:32.411 --> 00:08:35.714
You know, and my mom was always
trying give me solos and [bleep]

00:08:35.714 --> 00:08:37.416
Sorry, I cursed.
-That's all right.

00:08:37.416 --> 00:08:38.684
[ Laughter ]

00:08:38.684 --> 00:08:40.453
-So stressed out about that,
still, to this day.

00:08:40.453 --> 00:08:42.388
[ Laughter ]

00:08:42.388 --> 00:08:46.158
But report cards came,
the end of the year came,

00:08:46.158 --> 00:08:48.494
and, like, I'm thinking like,
"Okay.

00:08:48.494 --> 00:08:50.429
My mom is gonna give me
an A-plus."

00:08:50.429 --> 00:08:54.066
You know, it's my --
it's my ma, right?

00:08:54.066 --> 00:08:57.570
And I get my report card,
and I got a B-minus.

00:08:57.570 --> 00:09:01.507
[ Laughter ]

00:09:01.507 --> 00:09:03.809
-Yeah.
-Like, my mom keeps it so real.

00:09:03.809 --> 00:09:05.277
She was like,
"You missed some homework."

00:09:05.277 --> 00:09:07.145
[ Laughter ]

00:09:07.145 --> 00:09:09.414
I was like,
"You keep it so funky.

00:09:09.414 --> 00:09:11.617
I got to respect it."
-Well, absolutely.

00:09:11.617 --> 00:09:12.951
You set a bar high
for your kids.

00:09:12.951 --> 00:09:14.286
You got to do it.
You got to do it.

00:09:14.286 --> 00:09:15.954
-Yeah.
-You're gonna stick around.

00:09:15.954 --> 00:09:17.623
